'''twink'''<sup>2</sup> (twingk), ''n.'' [Cf. ''twink''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.,'' also ''pink, spink, finch,'' etc.] The chaffinch.

'''twinkle''' (twing′kl), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''twinkled,'' ppr. ''twinkling.'' [Early mod. E. ''twynkle, twynkell;'' < ME. ''twinclen, twynclen,'' < AS. ''twinclian,'' twinkle; freq. of *''twincan,'' wink: see ''twink''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To shut an eye or the eyes with an involuntary twitch or with a quick voluntary and significant action; blink; wink.
{{smaller block|She hath now ''twyncled'' fyrst upon the with wyckede eye.
{{right|''Chaucer,'' Boëthius, ii. prose 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|I ''twynkell'' with the eye. Je clignette. . . . You ''twynkell'' with your eye, do you? I truste you never the better.
{{right|''Palsgrave,'' p. 764.}}}}
{{smaller block|The owl fell a moping and ''twinkling.''}}

