'''twinkle''' (twing′kl), ''n.'' [< ''twinkle'', ''v.''] '''1.''' A twitching of the eyelid; a blinking; a wink.
{{smaller block|Old David moved from place to place about his ordinary employments, scarce shewing, unless by . . . an occasional convulsive sigh, or ''twinkle'' of the eyelid, that he was labouring under the yoke of such bitter affliction.{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xiv.}}}}
'''2.''' A quick, tremulous light; a glimmer; a sparkle; a flash.
{{smaller block|<poem>Glimmers and dies the fire-fly’s spark —
Like starry ''twinkles'' that momently break
Through the rifts of the gathering tempest’s rack.</poem>{{right|''J. R. Drake'', Culprit Fay.}}}}
'''3.''' The time required for a wink; a twinkling.

'''twinkler''' (twing′klėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''twynclere'' (= MHG. ''zwinkeler''); < ''twinkle'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which twinkles. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') A winker; a blinker; especially, the eye.}}
{{smaller block|The ''twynclere'' with the eȝe forgeth wicke thingus.{{right|''Wyclif'', Ecclus. xxvii. 25.}}}}
{{smaller block|You’ll just be pleased . . . not to be staring at me, following me up and down with those ''twinklers'' of yours.{{right|''Marryatt'', Snarleyyow, I. vii.}}}}

